range over a channel is a receive loop the compiler writes for you:

```go
for v := range ch {
    process(v)
}
```

Each iteration is a receive; the loop ends when the channel delivers the end-signal — closed and drained. There is NO other exit: no condition clause, no break-on-error, nothing to configure. The loop either runs until close or blocks forever on an empty open channel.

That's why range over channels requires the close discipline: the producer MUST close (typically defer close at the top of the producing function), or every downstream range leaks into a permanent block. The pairing is the idiom:

```go
func produce(out chan<- int) {
    defer close(out)  // the range's termination signal
    for _, v := range data {
        out <- v
    }
}

// consumer
for v := range out {   // terminates exactly when produce returns
    use(v)
}
```

The mechanics under the loop: range is the comma-ok receive — each iteration does v, ok := <-ch; ok true → body; ok false → exit. Buffered values drain normally (ok true for each); the close is seen only when the buffer empties. So range gets the lossless-drain-then-terminate guarantee the close semantics provide.

Where range DOESN'T fit: when the consumer has multiple channels, needs timeouts, or wants to stop early — those are select's jobs, with the comma-ok form explicitly written. range is the single-channel, run-to-completion loop — the simplest correct consumer, provided the producer owns the close.

break works exactly as you'd hope:

```go
for v := range ch {
    if v == stopValue {
        break   // leaves the range; ch is still open
    }
    process(v)
}
```

The range ends, but the channel's life continues. The consequences differ by what's upstream:

Unbuffered channel with a sender blocked trying to deliver: your break leaves that sender parked on its send — a goroutine leak. The sender needs to see a cancellation (a done channel, a context) to give up.

Buffered channel: subsequent sends fill the buffer (up to capacity), then senders block. Values you didn't consume are discarded when the channel is garbage-collected — but a sender blocked on a full channel leaks exactly as the unbuffered case.

So break answers YOUR side ('stop consuming') but not THEIRS ('stop producing'). The complete early-exit pattern pairs the break with a cancellation the producer selects on:

```go
done := make(chan struct{})
// producer: for { select { case out <- v: case <-done: return } }

for v := range out {
    if !keepGoing(v) {
        close(done)  // tell the producer
        break        // and leave
    }
}
```

That's the two-sided exit: consumer breaks, producer observes the done close, unwinds. context.Context is this same pattern, standardized — Done() is the done channel, and the idiom above is why ctx appears in every pipeline signature.

The two forms, side by side, doing the same job:

```go
// range — the specialist
for v := range ch {
    process(v)
}

// for + receive — the general form
for {
    v, ok := <-ch
    if !ok {
        break
    }
    process(v)
}
```

The range is the second loop, compiled: receive, check ok, run body, exit on end-signal. Every behavior difference falls out of that:

range has no per-iteration condition — you can't 'stop when v satisfies X' inside the range declaration itself (break in the body does it, but then the loop's exit is two mechanisms: close OR break). The for form has one explicit exit you fully own.

range can't select — one channel only. The for form composes with anything: select across multiple channels, timeouts, comma-ok — the full consumer toolkit.

The practical divide: range for the standard pipeline consumer — single upstream channel, run until the producer closes, the 90% case; it's less code and reads as 'drain this source.' The explicit loop when the consumer is a coordinator: multiple sources, deadlines, early exits, per-receive branching beyond processing. Code review often flags the reverse: an explicit comma-ok loop with no special logic is noise — range says it cleaner — and a range with three breaks and a condition hidden in the body is a loop that wants to be explicit.
